<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>A borehole pump fills the school's water tank in <m>{4}</m> hours. The kitchen taps, on their own, empty the full tank in <m>{6}</m> hours. The tank is empty, and the pump and the taps are turned on together.</p><p>Akinyi says: <q>The tank fills in <m>{6} - {4} = {2}</m> hours.</q></p><p>(a) Which statement is right?</p><p/><ol marker="(1)">
<li>Akinyi is wrong: with both on, the tank fills in 4 + 6 = 10 hours.</li>
<li>Akinyi is wrong: each hour the tank gains only <m>\frac{1}{4} - \frac{1}{6}</m> of its volume, so it takes longer than 4 hours.</li>
<li>Akinyi is right: the taps take away the difference between the two times.</li>
<li>Akinyi is wrong: the tank never fills, because water leaves as fast as it comes in.</li>
</ol>  <p>(b) How long does the tank take to fill? Give your answer exactly or correct to 1 decimal place.</p><p><fillin characters="6" name="ans2"/> hours  </p></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>The outlet takes away its fraction each hour. Each hour the pump adds <m>\dfrac{1}{ {4} }</m> of the tank and the taps remove <m>\dfrac{1}{ {6} }</m>, so the tank gains</p><p><me>\frac{1}{ {4} } - \frac{1}{ {6} } = {\frac{1}{12}}</me></p><p>of its volume each hour. It fills in <m>1 \div {\frac{1}{12}} = {12}</m> hours.</p><p>Akinyi's <m>{2}</m> hours is less than the <m>{4}</m> hours the pump needs with the taps off, so it cannot be right: opening the taps can only slow the filling down.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>(2)</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>12</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>